## Why This Is Asked
This question tests knowledge of 1D interval stabbing with dynamic updates and top-k queries across overlapping intervals—a common pattern in calendars, ad slot management, and reservation systems.

## Key Concepts
- **Segment tree**: Enables O(log N) node updates for interval operations and point queries
- **Per-node weight multisets**: Maintain counts of overlapping weights using BST/treap for O(log N) rank queries
- **Path aggregation**: Combines structures along the root-to-leaf path to answer sum and kth-largest queries

## Data Layout
1. **Coordinate compression**: Map all interval endpoints to sorted indices (0 to N-1)
2. **Segment tree nodes**: Each node stores a BST of (weight, count) pairs and nodeSum (total weight in that segment)
3. **Query paths**: Both sumWeightsAt and kthLargestWeightAt traverse O(log N) nodes along the path to point x

## Code Example
```javascript
class IntervalSet {
  constructor(points) {
    this.coords = this.compress(points);
    this.tree = this.build(0, this.coords.length - 1);
  }

  compress(points) {
    return [...new Set(points)].sort((a, b) => a - b);
  }

  insertInterval(L, R, w) {
    const l = this.lowerBound(L), r = this.lowerBound(R);
    this.update(this.tree.root, l, r, w, 1);
  }

  eraseInterval(L, R, w) {
    const l = this.lowerBound(L), r = this.lowerBound(R);
    this.update(this.tree.root, l, r, w, -1);
  }

  update(node, L, R, w, delta) {
    if (!node || L > node.r || R < node.l) return;
    if (L <= node.l && node.r <= R) {
      node.bst.insert(w, delta);
      node.sum += w * delta;
      return;
    }
    this.update(node.left, L, R, w, delta);
    this.update(node.right, L, R, w, delta);
  }

  sumWeightsAt(x) {
    const idx = this.lowerBound(x);
    return this.querySum(this.tree.root, idx);
  }

  querySum(node, idx) {
    if (!node) return 0;
    let res = node.sum;
    const mid = Math.floor((node.l + node.r) / 2);
    if (idx <= mid) res += this.querySum(node.left, idx);
    else res += this.querySum(node.right, idx);
    return res;
  }

  kthLargestWeightAt(x, k) {
    const idx = this.lowerBound(x);
    return this.queryKth(this.tree.root, idx, k);
  }

  queryKth(node, idx, k) {
    if (!node) return null;
    const counts = this.getPathCounts(node, idx);
    let lo = 0, hi = this.maxWeight;
    while (lo < hi) {
      const mid = Math.ceil((lo + hi) / 2);
      if (this.countGTE(counts, mid) >= k) lo = mid;
      else hi = mid - 1;
    }
    return lo;
  }
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- **insertInterval/eraseInterval**: O(log N) nodes × O(log M) BST ops = O(log² N)
- **sumWeightsAt**: O(log N) path traversal
- **kthLargestWeightAt**: O(log N) × O(log maxWeight) = O(log² N)

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design an order-statistics structure with duplicates using a compact, well-known DS (Fenwick). Requires handling inserts, deletes, and k-th retrieval within tight bounds.

## Key Concepts

- Coordinate compression to bound domain size
- Fenwick Tree for prefix sums (counts)
- getKthSmallest via binary lifting on Fenwick
- Handling duplicates and safe deletes
- Time: O(log m) per operation, m distinct values

## Code Example

```javascript
class Fenwick {
  constructor(n){ this.n=n; this.f=new Array(n+1).fill(0); }
  add(i, delta){ for(; i<=this.n; i+= i&-i) this.f[i]+=delta; }
  sum(i){ let s=0; for(; i>0; i-= i&-i) s+=this.f[i]; return s; }
  kth(k){
    let idx=0; let bit=1;
    while((bit<<1) <= this.n) bit <<= 1;
    for(let step=bit; step>0; step>>=1){
      const t = idx+step;
      if(t<=this.n && this.f[t] < k){
        idx = t;
        k -= this.f[t];
      }
    }
    return idx+1;
  }
}
class KthMultiset {
  constructor(vals){
    this.values = Array.from(new Set(vals)).sort((a,b)=>a-b);
    this.index = new Map(this.values.map((v,i)=>[v,i+1]));
    this.ft = new Fenwick(this.values.length);
  }
  add(x){ const i = this.index.get(x); if(i) this.ft.add(i, 1); }
  remove(x){ const i = this.index.get(x); if(i) this.ft.add(i, -1); }
  getKthSmallest(k){ const idx = this.ft.kth(k); return this.values[idx-1]; }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to combine a hashmap with a priority structure to track a dynamic mode under duplicates, while keeping updates O(log n) on average. It also checks handling of stale entries and edge cases (removals of non-existent values).

## Key Concepts
- Hash map for value-to-count to enable O(1) updates
- Max-heap keyed by (count, -value) to prioritize higher counts and break ties toward smaller values
- Lazy deletion of stale heap entries to keep updates efficient
- Edge cases: removing non-existent values, empty multiset

## Code Example
```javascript
class ModeMultiset {
  constructor() {
    this.cnt = new Map();
    this.heap = []; // max-heap of [count, -value]
  }
  _heapPush(item) { /* standard binary-heap insert with comparator by count desc, value asc */ }
  _heapPop() { /* standard binary-heap extract-max */ }
  addValue(x) {
    const c = (this.cnt.get(x) || 0) + 1;
    this.cnt.set(x, c);
    this._heapPush([c, -x]);
  }
  removeValue(x) {
    const c = this.cnt.get(x);
    if (!c) return;
    const nc = c - 1;
    if (nc > 0) this.cnt.set(x, nc);
    else this.cnt.delete(x);
    if (nc > 0) this._heapPush([nc, -x]);
  }
  getMode() {
    while (this.heap.length) {
      const [c, neg] = this.heap[0];
      const v = -neg;
      if ((this.cnt.get(v) || 0) === c) return v;
      this._heapPop();
    }
    return null;
  }
}
```

